One common way to introduce vulnerabilities is through **Insecure Deserialization**. When an application uses `ObjectInputStream` to deserialize untrusted data from a user without validation, an attacker can supply a malicious serialized object to execute arbitrary code (RCE) on the server.

```java
import java.io.*;
import java.net.ServerSocket;
import java.net.Socket;

public class VulnerableServer {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws IOException {
        ServerSocket serverSocket = new ServerSocket(8080);
        System.out.println("Server listening on port 8080...");

        while (true) {
            try (Socket socket = serverSocket.accept();
                 ObjectInputStream in = new ObjectInputStream(socket.getInputStream())) {

                // This line is vulnerable: it deserializes any object sent by the client,
                // which can lead to Remote Code Execution (RCE) via gadget chains.
                Object obj = in.readObject();
                System.out.println("Received object: " + obj.toString());
            } catch (Exception e) {
                e.printStackTrace();
            }
        }
    }
}
```

