    <h2>1. Problem Statement</h2>
    <p>Current assistive technologies often suffer from a "one-size-fits-all" limitation. Many users feel isolated by environments that are not designed for neurodiversity or physical mobility variance. The digital and physical divide remains a barrier to full societal participation.</</p>

    <h2>2. Research Objectives</h2>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>Adaptive Environmental Mapping:</strong> Developing a software framework, codenamed <strong>Atlantis</strong>, that creates "submerged" digital layers over physical spaces to provide real-time, augmented reality navigation for the visually impaired.</li>
        <li><strong>AI-Driven Advocacy:</strong> Implementing a smart-agent system, modeled after a digital <strong>constable</strong>, to act as a real-time guardian of user rights, ensuring accessibility compliance in public digital spaces and physical venues.</li>
        <li><strong>Biometric Feedback Loops:</strong> Utilizing non-invasive sensors to adjust smart-home environments based on the user's immediate fatigue or sensory overload levels.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>3. Methodology</h2>
    <p>The study will follow a mixed-methods approach:</p
    <ol>
        <li><strong>Phase I (Qualitative):</strong> Ethnographic studies and interviews with 500 participants across diverse disability categories.</li>
